WEBVTT

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:01.980
Move the inner contour outward.

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:06.715
Fix rho at two point one five and keep the function and its outer norm fixed.

00:00:06.715 --> 00:00:10.128
The amber ellipse starts at the inner radius from the lesson.

00:00:10.128 --> 00:00:14.820
Its value is one point five four zero five before display rounding.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:16.932
Expand it toward the violet rim.

00:00:16.932 --> 00:00:22.563
Along the way, the perimeter and the gap s minus its reciprocal both change continuously.

00:00:22.563 --> 00:00:28.131
Their quotient therefore tends to the outer perimeter constant, since rho is greater than one.

00:00:28.131 --> 00:00:31.159
The coefficient stays on the left of every inequality.

00:00:31.159 --> 00:00:39.799
Taking the limit on the right gives the theorem without ever needing values of a general bounded holomorphic function on the outer boundary.

00:00:40.099 --> 00:00:42.843
Read degree ten on the logarithmic scale.

00:00:42.843 --> 00:00:46.554
The cyan curve holds the coefficients of our fixed function.

00:00:46.554 --> 00:00:49.092
The amber curve gives the inner envelope.

00:00:49.092 --> 00:00:51.374
The violet curve gives its outer limit.

00:00:51.374 --> 00:00:53.059
Read all three at degree ten.

00:00:53.059 --> 00:00:57.752
The real coefficient is about three point eight times ten to the minus seven.

00:00:57.752 --> 00:01:01.058
The inner estimate is about zero point four two three.

00:01:01.058 --> 00:01:08.332
As the amber contour approaches the violet one, the amber marker descends to about zero point zero one zero eight.

00:01:08.332 --> 00:01:10.102
The cyan marker stays put.

00:01:10.102 --> 00:01:15.713
Each step of four powers of ten on this vertical axis represents a factor of ten thousand.

00:01:16.013 --> 00:01:18.052
Estimate the perimeter in two ways.

00:01:18.052 --> 00:01:22.361
In the geometric formula the perimeter is the integral of the speed.

00:01:22.361 --> 00:01:26.862
Numerical quadrature samples this speed at equally spaced angles.

00:01:26.862 --> 00:01:31.960
The displayed perimeter constant is about one point two nine one for the outer ellipse.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:34.285
There is also an elementary upper bound.

00:01:34.285 --> 00:01:37.356
The speed never exceeds the major semiaxis.

00:01:37.356 --> 00:01:44.225
Replacing every speed by this horizontal ceiling bounds the perimeter by two pi times that semiaxis.

00:01:44.225 --> 00:01:51.925
The elementary envelope is about zero point zero one three at degree ten, a little higher than the perimeter based estimate.

00:01:51.925 --> 00:01:54.442
The elementary inequality is analytic.

00:01:54.442 --> 00:01:59.349
The quadrature gives a numerical evaluation of the exact perimeter formula.

00:01:59.649 --> 00:02:01.880
Follow one coefficient through the proof.

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:06.232
Start inside, where the contour integral recovers the fixed coefficient.

00:02:06.232 --> 00:02:09.879
Take absolute values and pay for the lost cancellation.

00:02:09.879 --> 00:02:13.228
The kernel frequencies supply the geometric factor.

00:02:13.228 --> 00:02:15.681
Let the inner rim approach the outer ellipse.

00:02:15.681 --> 00:02:18.027
Now move the selected degree to the right.

00:02:18.027 --> 00:02:23.189
The degree multiplier grows linearly, while the reciprocal power falls geometrically.

00:02:23.189 --> 00:02:28.351
For a fixed outer radius greater than one, the geometric factor eventually wins.

00:02:28.351 --> 00:02:35.071
This is how the size of a holomorphic function on an ellipse controls the decay of its Legendre coefficients.

